<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What can be built, and what out of.

  TWO TABLES, NOT ONE. A wall is a wall whether it is wood or stone, and wood is wood whether it
  is a wall or a door. Folding them into one table of "wooden wall, stone wall" looks simpler
  while there are two of each and multiplies the moment there are six. It is also the shape
  PlacedEdifice has carried since the ruined city was written: a def and a stuff, side by side.

  THE HANDLE ORDER IS THE CONTRACT, as it is for terrain. A BuildingDef's position is a
  BuildingHandle value and a StuffDef's is a StuffHandle value; both are written into the
  published frame and into every save, so a row that moved would make an existing colony read as
  building something else. ConstructionContent's in-code tables are the oracle and
  ConstructionContentDefTests walks the two against each other field by field.

  FACTORS ARE IN THOUSANDTHS, for the reason skill experience is: a factor multiplied into a tick
  count has to give the same answer on every machine, and integer arithmetic is the only way to
  promise that. Nothing here is a float, so it all hashes without a rounding question.
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- 0 is "nothing", matching the grid's zero default, exactly as TerrainAir is 0. -->
  <BuildingDef>
    <defName>Building_None</defName>
    <label>nothing</label>
    <edifice>0</edifice>
  </BuildingDef>

  <!--
    costCount 5 and workToBuild 135 are the reference's own wall (a-04-building-and-materials.md
    section 6). The cost is quoted in STUFF, so a wooden wall costs five wood and a stone wall five
    stone rather than five of some third thing.

    135 ticks is deliberately short beside felling's 800: the interesting part of building a wall
    is fetching the wood for it, and a wall that took a morning to raise would make a hut a week's
    work. edifice 1 is CoreContent.EdificeWall - the same record the ruined city stamps, so a wall
    a colonist built and a wall the generator laid are indistinguishable to the mesher, the picker
    and the support solver.
  -->
  <BuildingDef>
    <defName>Building_Wall</defName>
    <label>wall</label>
    <edifice>1</edifice>
    <blocking>true</blocking>
    <costCount>5</costCount>
    <workToBuild>135</workToBuild>
    <minSkill>0</minSkill>
    <iconKey>ui.arch.tool.wall</iconKey>
  </BuildingDef>

  <!--
    U29. A roof is a floor is a slab: one thing, of a material, at the boundary between two layers,
    stored on the upper cell. So this is not a second pipeline - it is Building_Wall with one field
    changed, and `slab` is that field.

    costCount 4 and workToBuild 120 against a wall's 5 and 135: slightly less of both, because a
    slab is less material than a 3 m wall and because the interesting part of building either is
    fetching the stuff. Neither number is derived from anything and nothing derives from them; they
    are the owner's to tune.

    blocking false: you walk on a floor. edifice 0, because a floor is not an edifice at all -
    ConstructionGrid.Raise writes CoreContent.SlabBuilt into Floor[cell] and the material into
    FloorStuff[cell], and the cell itself stays empty.

    iconKey is the catalogue's existing ui.arch.tool.roof. The key is unchanged and only its label
    moved, because icon-map.csv is keyed the same way and a key is forever.
  -->
  <BuildingDef>
    <defName>Building_Floor</defName>
    <label>floor</label>
    <edifice>0</edifice>
    <slab>true</slab>
    <blocking>false</blocking>
    <costCount>4</costCount>
    <workToBuild>120</workToBuild>
    <minSkill>0</minSkill>
    <iconKey>ui.arch.tool.roof</iconKey>
  </BuildingDef>

  <!--
    U42. A floor you lay on ground that is already there, which is what "just build a floor" means
    and is NOT what Building_Floor is. The two are one field apart and opposite about one question:
    a floor wants a cell with nothing under it and must satisfy the support rule; a deck plate wants
    a cell that is already floored and asks the support rule nothing, because the ground is holding
    it up and it can never fall. docs/design/18-paving.md has the measurement that produced it.

    costCount 3 and workToBuild 60, the cheapest and quickest thing in the table: it is a surface,
    not a structure, it carries no load and it is laid over an area rather than a line. Against a
    floor's 4 and 120 and a wall's 5 and 135. Neither number derives from anything and nothing
    derives from them; they are the owner's to tune.

    edifice 0 and blocking false, as a floor is: nothing stands in the cell, and you walk on it.
    slab true because it IS a slab - written to the cell's lower boundary through exactly the same
    Raise - and covering true is the one field that inverts the rule.

    Built of wood or stone like everything else, because those are the only two stuffs a colonist
    can carry (ConstructionContent.IsBuildable). The four with no item are what the ruined city is
    made of, and steel is one of them - so a steel deck plate is an order that could never be
    filled until a salvage line gives steel an item.

    One covering rather than three. Grating and tile are a row each when they are wanted, and
    grating is not a covering at all - "see and fall through" is a slab you can see through, which
    wants something below worth seeing.
  -->
  <BuildingDef>
    <defName>Building_DeckPlate</defName>
    <label>deck plate</label>
    <edifice>0</edifice>
    <slab>true</slab>
    <covering>true</covering>
    <blocking>false</blocking>
    <costCount>3</costCount>
    <workToBuild>60</workToBuild>
    <minSkill>0</minSkill>
    <iconKey>ui.arch.tool.deckplate</iconKey>
  </BuildingDef>

  <!--
    U43. The way up, and the first thing a colony can build that goes anywhere but sideways.

    Until this existed a second storey was decorative: every slab measured walkable and
    UNREACHABLE, because vertical movement in this game goes through a Pathing.Connector and
    connectors only ever came out of worldgen. A ladder is the cheapest honest fix - one cell,
    joining the floor it stands on to the floor directly above it.

    blocking false, and that is the whole of what makes it work: a ladder you cannot stand in is
    a decoration. edifice 7 is CoreContent.EdificeLadder, the same record the ruined city stamps,
    so a ladder a colonist built and a ladder the generator left are the same thing to the mesher,
    the picker and the nav graph.

    costCount 4 and workToBuild 90: between a floor's 4/120 and a wall's 5/135. It is less material
    than a wall and quicker to put up than a floor, and like both of those neither number derives
    from anything. The owner's to tune.

    Stairs are deliberately NOT here yet. A stair is two cells and rises 1.5 m per cell, so it
    carries a placement rule of its own; a ladder needs none.
  -->
  <BuildingDef>
    <defName>Building_Ladder</defName>
    <label>ladder</label>
    <edifice>7</edifice>
    <blocking>false</blocking>
    <costCount>4</costCount>
    <workToBuild>90</workToBuild>
    <minSkill>0</minSkill>
    <iconKey>ui.arch.tool.ladder</iconKey>
  </BuildingDef>

  <!--
    The four stuffs with no item are what the ruined city is MADE OF, not what a colony builds
    with: nothing produces them, no colonist can carry one, and offering them would be offering an
    order that can never be filled. They keep their indices, because every stamped wall in the city
    carries one, and ConstructionContent.IsBuildable is what keeps them off the menu. A salvage
    line that turned rubble into steel would give one an item and put it there, with no other
    change anywhere.
  -->
  <StuffDef>
    <defName>Stuff_None</defName>
    <label>nothing</label>
    <stuff>0</stuff>
    <item>-1</item>
  </StuffDef>

  <StuffDef>
    <defName>Stuff_Concrete</defName>
    <label>concrete</label>
    <stuff>1</stuff>
    <item>-1</item>
  </StuffDef>

  <StuffDef>
    <defName>Stuff_Steel</defName>
    <label>steel</label>
    <stuff>2</stuff>
    <item>-1</item>
  </StuffDef>

  <StuffDef>
    <defName>Stuff_Composite</defName>
    <label>composite</label>
    <stuff>3</stuff>
    <item>-1</item>
  </StuffDef>

  <!--
    item 2 is ItemHandle.Wood: what felling a tree leaves on the ground.

    workOffsetTicks 0 (U27): nailing and lashing a plank into place has no separate fitting step
    for the factor to leave out, so the whole of wood's cost is the factor.
  -->
  <StuffDef>
    <defName>Stuff_Wood</defName>
    <label>wood</label>
    <stuff>4</stuff>
    <item>2</item>
    <workFactorPerMille>1000</workFactorPerMille>
    <workOffsetTicks>0</workOffsetTicks>
    <hitPointsFactorPerMille>1000</hitPointsFactorPerMille>
    <iconKey>ui.res.wood</iconKey>
  </StuffDef>

  <!--
    item 3 is ItemHandle.Stone: what mining a rock cell leaves.

    1.7x the work and 1.5x the hit points is the reference's own relation between a wooden wall and
    a stone one (a-04 section 3, read off its material table). It is the one number that makes the
    choice of material a decision rather than a colour, which is why it is carried in ahead of the
    rest of the stat block.

    workOffsetTicks 15 (U27, stat = base x factor + offset, a-04 section 3) is Odyssey's own
    number: a flat dressing-and-fitting pass a stone block wants regardless of the wall it joins,
    on top of the factor above. Proportionate to the one factor Odyssey chose rather than copied
    from the reference's own much larger stone offset (+140 against a base of 135, stacked with a
    x5-6 factor across several stone materials).
  -->
  <StuffDef>
    <defName>Stuff_Stone</defName>
    <label>stone</label>
    <stuff>5</stuff>
    <item>3</item>
    <workFactorPerMille>1700</workFactorPerMille>
    <workOffsetTicks>15</workOffsetTicks>
    <hitPointsFactorPerMille>1500</hitPointsFactorPerMille>
    <iconKey>ui.res.stone</iconKey>
  </StuffDef>

</Defs>
